WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.202
-Our next guest won Best Actress

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:03.903
at Cannes Film Festival
for her work

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.171
in the critically acclaimed film

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.639
"The Worst Person in the World,"

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.708
which is in theaters
February 4th.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:09.709
Let's take a look.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.462
Please welcome to the show
Renate Reinsve.

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:32.465
[ Cheering and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:41.407 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:49.315 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.084
Welcome to the show!
-Thank you.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.085
Thank you.
-Congratulations on this movie.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:54.653
This is a wonderful film.

00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:55.788
It's a beautiful film.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.657
It tells the story
of a fascinating woman

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:01.094
and you won Best Actress
at Cannes.

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.528
What has this journey been like?

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:05.464
-It's been something.

00:01:05.464 --> 00:01:07.533
[ Laughter ]
I don't --

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.402
I can't put it into words, yet,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.004
because it's like
I'm sitting here now.

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:13.672
It's terrifying.
It's wonderful.

00:01:13.672 --> 00:01:15.708
Like it's all the words.
I don't know, yeah.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.309
[ Laughter ]
-The thing I keep reading --

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:18.411
and I'm wondering
if it's true --

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.779
that you had decided,
the day before --

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.047
This is your first lead role.

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.148
The day before you were offered
this film,

00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:24.984
you had decided to quit acting.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:28.321
-Yes, I had like this big moment
where I laid in bed,

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.256
and I kind of like

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:33.059
this big moment of, "I'm done.
I'm finished."

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.327
And then, the day after,
he called me for this role.

00:01:35.327 --> 00:01:37.463
It's very strange.
Weird.

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:39.899
-What was your backup plan?

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:40.833
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.435
It was carpenting.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.636
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.471
So almost the same as acting.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.640
[ Laughter ]
Almost the same.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.409
-So this is a director,
Joachim Trier --

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:52.045
I think I'm saying that right?
-Yes, you were.

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:55.447
-You had been
in a previous movie of his,

00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:57.884
called "Oslo August 31st,"
something like that?

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:00.019
-Yeah.
-Beautiful film as well.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.355
You had one line in that film.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:03.889
-You must've made
quite an impact.

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:04.990
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:06.292
-Do you want to do
the line for us?

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.326
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:08.527
-[ U.S. accent ]
Let's go to the party.

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:09.662
-So good.
-Yeah.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:10.930
[ Laughter and applause ]
-Thanks.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.331
-So good.
[ Cheering ]

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.866
I mean, I remember.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:18.237
-But, recently, someone told me
I also have another line

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:20.039
that I forgot.
It was improvised.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:21.540
It's "I have water in my nose."

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.175
Also a great line.
-Wow!

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:24.711
[ Laughter ]
It wasn't even written for you.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.412
-No!
-That's what you bring

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.348
to a project.
-I know!

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:29.081
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:30.250
-Now, I'm wondering --

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.784
because he came to you and said,

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.553
"I wrote this part for you."

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:35.488
-Yeah.
-The the movie is called

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.488
"The Worst Person in the World."

00:02:36.488 --> 00:02:39.592
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:42.461
-I mean, that must've been
a real like good news, bad news.

00:02:42.461 --> 00:02:44.297
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:45.798
The first thing I thought was,

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.900
"My ex is going to be
very happy."

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.269
[ Laughter ]
My face is going to be
on a poster

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:52.571
and it's going to be
"The Worst Person in the World."

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:53.572
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:54.406
-I didn't even think of that.

00:02:54.406 --> 00:02:55.341
He must've been like,

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.142
"What did I tell you?"
-Yes.

00:02:57.142 --> 00:03:00.279
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:02.215
-You're obviously not
the worst person in the world.

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:05.151
In this film, you are, I think,
a complicated person,

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.153
which are the most
interesting to play.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.621
It's a beautifully shot film.

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:12.491
There's a moment where everyone
freezes and you run through.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:14.260
You're in motion.
Everyone else is frozen.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.361
Not CGI, correct?
-No, nothing.

00:03:16.361 --> 00:03:17.796
He wanted to do it
like old-school.

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:19.499
Joachim, I mean, yeah.

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:21.400
-So what does that mean,
that a bunch of other

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.569
sort of secondary actors,
extras,

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:25.437
have to just
stand perfectly still?

00:03:25.437 --> 00:03:27.674
-Well, they were like
rehearsing.

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.975
They were standing there,
looked very good.

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:31.110
You've seen it, you know.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.878
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah. And I was running

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:37.450
up and down and then, suddenly,
we saw more people joined in.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.852
So, and then they had
to stay the whole day.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.220
[ Laughter ]
And so we actually got

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.422
more people in during the day.

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:45.991
-So this is just people walking
around Oslo who are like,

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.627
"Alright, I'll stand still
for a big movie"?

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.028
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah. They were annoyed

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.529
because we had to like
stop traffic.

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:52.398
Oslo is very small, so,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.468
if you block off a street,
it's like half the city.

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:58.203
[ Laughter ]
So, everyone was like waiting

00:03:58.203 --> 00:04:01.173
for us to finish
so they can cross the street

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:03.542
and then they just joined in,
instead, yeah.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:04.977
[ Laughter ]
-Now, I would imagine

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.479
maybe they're frustrated
at the time

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:07.880
because their street
gets shut down.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.851
But, for a Norwegian film
to get international acclaim

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.954
and attention like this has,
are people in Norway

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.090
intensely proud of how
the film's been received?

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.290
-Yeah, so proud.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:21.027
Like I feel that we all --

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.262
Like Norway did it.
Now, we're like --

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.930
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.265
We -- Yes.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.200
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Yeah, give it up for Norway.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.903
-Everyone is happy.
[ Laughs ]

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.905
-I mean, when you go
to someplace like Cannes,

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.874
do you get to meet, run into,

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.376
other actors that you've
respected over the years?

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.912
-Yeah. I, uh -- [ Laughs ]

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:42.382
Yeah.
I had this moment.

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.650
I think it was the day
after we showed the film

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.020
and I walked past
Tilda Swinton.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.621
She's, you know.
-That's a real good one.

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:52.592
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
And then I walked past

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:55.360
and then I kind of backed up
because I was, "Is that her?"

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.465
And I turned and I looked
and I waited until she saw me

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.099
and I gave her like a bow.

00:05:01.099 --> 00:05:02.568
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.037
I don't know what.

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:07.807
And then, I thought
she was going to be like,

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:10.542
"Oh, thank you,"
but she was like this --

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:13.012
[ Laughter ]
And then I heard
she just saw the movie.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.280
-Right.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.549
-I ran out after that, yeah.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:19.051
-That's very Cinderella.
-Yes.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:20.386
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.687
-You deserve bows as well.

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.222
Congratulations on the film
and thank you so much

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.024
for making time for us.
-Thank you so much
for having me.

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:25.791
[ Cheering and applause ]
Thank you.
-You guys, that's

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:27.693
Renata Reinsve, everybody!

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:30.730
"The Worst Person in the World"
is in theaters February 4th.

